#: includes/settings-page.php:580
msgid "Settings on this screen that apply only to the comment form and the REST API comments endpoint. Thresholds shared with every other guarded form (Login, CF7, WooCommerce, and the rest) live in Timing & Token Engine below."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:586
msgid "Enable Init Void Shield"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:592
msgid "Activate the honeypot defense on comment forms."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:600
msgid "Apply to Logged-in Users"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:606
msgid "Also verify comments from logged-in users."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:609
msgid "By default, logged-in users are trusted and bypass all checks. Enable this if your site has open registration or untrusted members."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:617
msgid "Block REST API Comments"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:623
msgid "Reject comments submitted directly through the REST API (wp/v2/comments)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:626
msgid "The honeypot and JS token checks above only cover the classic comment form; REST API submissions bypass them entirely. Enable this only if your site does not rely on a headless app or other legitimate client that posts comments via the REST API."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:634
msgid "Require Same-Site Referer"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:640
msgid "Reject a comment submission whose Referer header is missing or points to a different site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:643
msgid "Catches a scripted bot that posts directly to the comment endpoint without ever loading the page. Off by default: some privacy-focused browsers and extensions strip the Referer header even on a real, same-site submission, which this cannot tell apart from a bot. Enable only after confirming it does not affect real visitors on your site, and use it alongside the other layers, not instead of them."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:651
msgid "Maximum Links per Comment"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:660
msgid "links"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:662
msgid "Reject a comment containing more links than this. Set to 0 (the default) to disable. Unlike the checks above, this and the content filters below read what the commenter actually wrote, so they also catch spam posted by a real person or a real browser. They also apply to comments that bypass the honeypot checks, such as wpDiscuz comments."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:670
msgid "Block BBCode Links"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:676
msgid "Reject a comment containing a [url]...[/url] or [link]...[/link] BBCode link."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:679
msgid "WordPress never renders BBCode, so a real commenter gains nothing from it; it is a signature of spam tools built for forums. Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:687
msgid "Block URLs in Author Name"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:693
msgid "Reject a comment whose Name field contains a web address."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:696
msgid "A classic way to sneak a keyword link into the comment byline. The Website field is not affected. Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:703
#: includes/settings-page.php:707
msgid "Block Comments in Selected Scripts"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:716
msgid "Reject a comment when a real share of its letters (30% by default), or of its author name, is written in one of the checked scripts. Useful when your readers never write in them but spam often does. A genuine comment that only quotes a foreign name or word is not affected, and Latin-script languages (English, Vietnamese, French, ...) never are. Nothing is checked by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:724
msgid "Allow Pingbacks & Trackbacks"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:730
msgid "Let pingbacks and trackbacks through the comment guard."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:733
msgid "Pingbacks and trackbacks are sent by other servers, not typed into your comment form, so they can never pass the honeypot checks and are blocked while the guard is enabled; most of them are spam. Enable this only if you want to receive them. They are then left to the WordPress discussion settings and moderation queue. Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:739
msgid "Timing & Token Engine"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:741
msgid "These thresholds are shared by the core engine: comments, the WordPress core forms below, and every form plugin integration on this page all use them, not comments alone."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:747
msgid "Minimum Submit Time"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:756
#: includes/settings-page.php:775
#: includes/settings-page.php:813
msgid "seconds"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:758
msgid "Comments and other content-style forms (topics, replies, contact forms) submitted faster than this will be rejected. Humans need time to read and type. Login, registration, and other account forms use the separate setting below instead."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:766
msgid "Account Forms Minimum Submit Time"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:777
msgid "Separate, shorter floor used by the Login, Registration, Lost Password, Multisite Signup, WooCommerce account forms, and BuddyPress registration guards instead of the general Minimum Submit Time above. A browser autofilling a saved username and password lets a genuine visitor submit far faster than someone typing a comment from scratch, so these forms need a lower bar. Set to 0 to disable this specific check for account forms while keeping every other layer (honeypot fields, JS/headless detection, signed time token) fully active."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:785
msgid "JavaScript Token Delay"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:794
msgid "milliseconds"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:796
msgid "Delay before the hidden JS token is injected. Catches headless browsers that submit instantly. A small random amount of extra time is always added on top of this value so the exact wait cannot be read from the page source and timed around. On login, registration, comment, and other forms that submit without AJAX, a visitor who submits before the token is ready (for example right after the browser autofills a saved password) is briefly held until it is, instead of being rejected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:804
msgid "Maximum Token Age"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:815
msgid "A submission carrying a token older than this is rejected, so a token cannot be captured once and replayed indefinitely. On a site with full-page caching, a token is baked in at the moment a page is cached, not the moment a visitor actually loads it — if comments from real visitors are being rejected as expired, raise this to comfortably cover your cache lifetime, or enable Lazy Fetch below instead of raising it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:821
msgid "WordPress Core Forms"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:823
msgid "Optional honeypot protection for the default WordPress login, registration, and lost-password forms at wp-login.php. Disabled by default, since these guard authentication itself — review your setup after enabling."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:829
msgid "Guard Login Form"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:835
msgid "Protect the default WP login form against automated login attempts."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:843
msgid "Login Guard Scope"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:848
msgid "Everywhere (wp-login.php + any front-end wp_login_form() usage)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:849
msgid "wp-login.php only"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:852
msgid "If your theme or a plugin renders a login form on the front end with wp_login_form() (e.g. a login page, widget, or modal) and that page may be served by a full-page cache, choose \"wp-login.php only\" to leave that specific form unguarded rather than risk real visitors being rejected. This uses the Referer header as a heuristic to tell the two forms apart, which a determined bot could spoof — the default (\"Everywhere\") gives the strongest protection and is recommended unless you hit this specific situation."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:860
msgid "Guard Registration Form"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:866
msgid "Protect the default WP registration form (wp-login.php?action=register)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:869
msgid "Only applies if user registration is open. Multisite signup (wp-signup.php) uses a different form and is not covered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:877
msgid "Guard Lost Password Form"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:883
msgid "Protect the default WP lost-password form."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:891
msgid "Guard Multisite Signup Form"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:897
msgid "Protect the site/user signup form at wp-signup.php."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:902
msgid "Multisite detected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:904
msgid "This site is not a Multisite network; this guard has no effect here."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:912
msgid "Form Plugin Integrations"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:914
msgid "Optional honeypot protection for popular third-party form plugins. Each toggle only takes effect if the corresponding plugin is active."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:926
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to all Contact Form 7 forms."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:941
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to all WPForms forms."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:956
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to all Gravity Forms forms."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:963
msgid "Community & E-commerce Integrations"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:965
msgid "Optional honeypot protection for WooCommerce, bbPress, and BuddyPress. Each toggle only takes effect if the corresponding plugin is active."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:971
msgid "WooCommerce Registration"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:977
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to the WooCommerce \"My Account\" registration form."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:986
msgid "WooCommerce Login"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:992
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to the WooCommerce login form (My Account page and the checkout login prompt)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:995
msgid "Independent from the core Login Guard, since WooCommerce uses its own login form and handler. Uses the Account Forms Minimum Submit Time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1004
msgid "WooCommerce Lost Password"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1010
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to the WooCommerce lost-password form."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1013
msgid "Always in effect while Guard Lost Password Form (under WordPress Core Forms) is enabled, since WooCommerce's reset handler runs the same core check."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1022
msgid "bbPress Topics & Replies"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1028
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to the New Topic and Reply forms."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1037
msgid "BuddyPress Registration"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1043
msgid "Add the honeypot guard to the BuddyPress signup form."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1050
msgid "Advanced Protection"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1055
msgid "Custom Field Prefix"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1065
msgid "Prefix used to build the hidden honeypot field names (lowercase letters, numbers, underscore only). Change this if you suspect a spammer has targeted your site specifically."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1073
msgid "CSS Trap Rotation"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1079
msgid "Randomize the CSS technique and field order used to hide honeypot traps on every render."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1082
msgid "Makes it harder for CSS-aware bots to learn a single fixed pattern. Traps remain invisible to humans either way."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1090
msgid "Headless Browser Detection"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1096
msgid "Flag common automation signals (navigator.webdriver, zero-size browser window, HeadlessChrome/PhantomJS, Selenium/ChromeDriver traces) picked up by the JS token script."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1099
msgid "Lightweight, client-side only, no external calls. Disable this if you use browser-automation tools to test submissions on your own site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1107
msgid "Block Non-Browser User Agents"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1113
msgid "Reject submissions whose User-Agent header identifies a known scripted HTTP client (curl, Python requests, Go, Scrapy, and similar)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1116
msgid "A real browser running the JS layer always sends its own browser User-Agent, so a match here is a near-zero-false-positive signal. Catches a bot that never runs JavaScript at all and simply replays the static form fields, which the honeypot and JS checks alone cannot see. On by default; the list of signatures is filterable for developers."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1124
msgid "Block Cross-Site Submissions"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1130
msgid "Reject a submission the browser itself reports as sent from a different website (Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1133
msgid "Browsers set this header themselves and page scripts cannot change it, so unlike the Referer header it is not stripped by privacy extensions. A real visitor using a form on this site (or on one of its subdomains) never sends it as cross-site. A request without the header, from an older browser for example, is never rejected by this check. Applies to every guarded form. On by default; only disable it if a form on an unrelated domain legitimately posts to this site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1141
msgid "Require Real User Interaction"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1147
msgid "Also require at least one real mouse, keyboard, touch, or scroll event before the form is accepted."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1150
msgid "Catches bots that simply wait out the JavaScript Token Delay instead of interacting with the page. An interaction is only counted after a short minimum delay of its own, so a bot cannot satisfy this by firing one synthetic event the instant the page loads. Off by default; works best with a JavaScript Token Delay of at least 1-2 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1158
msgid "Lazy Fetch (Cache-Safe Tokens)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1164
msgid "Refresh the time token via a small same-origin JavaScript request as soon as the page truly loads, instead of relying only on the value baked in when the page was rendered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/settings-page.php:1167
msgid "Recommended for sites with aggressive full-page caching. On such a site, the token baked into a cached page reflects the moment the page was cached, not the moment a real visitor loaded it, which can make legitimate comments look expired under a low Maximum Token Age. When enabled, this refreshes the token client-side (plain JavaScript, no jQuery) right after the page loads, so timing is always measured from the real visit — applies to every guarded form, not just comments. If the request fails or JavaScript is unavailable, the original baked-in token is used as a fallback, so this can only help. Off by default."
msgstr ""

